- Vervliet (2008), The Palaeotypography of the French Renaissance (Brill) (peer_reviewed) [Hendrik D. L. Vervliet, 'The Aldine italics', in The Palaeotypography of the French Renaissance: Selected Papers on Sixteenth‑Century Typefaces (Leiden: Brill, 2008).]: Type‑historical record: the fount cut by Francesco Griffo da Bologna, first seen 1500 and used as a complete book fount in 1501, was a 'cursive' (cancelleresca‑derived) type distinct from roman; as 'Aldine italic' it defined the look of the Aldine octavos and was the fount copied by the Giunti, the Lyonese counterfeiters, and later French punchcutters, making it the visual hallmark by which the new portable classics were identified.

