- Check 2 (Source fidelity): fail — The absence of citations means the claim has no support, but the claim is nonetheless affirmatively contradicted by the primary bibliographic records of published English translations; therefore the verdict is FAIL rather than merely UNSETTLED. The 1592 and 1999 translations are established bibliographic facts verifiable from publisher and national-library catalogues.
- Check 3 (Alias sweep): fail — Absence claim ('never translated') fails if anything surfaces under any alias. Sweep found English-titled edition records under 'Dream of Poliphilus' (1888, 1889 Open Library) and English-titled peer-reviewed items under 'Poliphilo' (1951 AJP and Artibus Asiae). Full-title alias sweep also performed and returned German catalogue/encyclopedia records. Together this contradicts 'never translated into English'.
- Check 4 (Right-level check): fail — Claim is a universal negative at work-level. Positive counter-example suffices to falsify. Opened publisher's official record and Library of Congress record showing a complete English edition trans. Joscelyn Godwin 1999, corroborated by peer-reviewed review in Renaissance Quarterly that treats it as the English translation, plus ESTC record of 1592 partial English translation The strife of love in a dreame. Existence of English translations directly contradicts 'never translated into English'.
