- Journal of the Society of Architectural Historians (peer_reviewed) [Roswitha Stewering, "Architectural Representations in the Hypnerotomachia Poliphili (Aldus Manutius, 1499)," Journal of the Society of Architectural Historians 59, no. 1 (2000): 6-25.]: The critical edition's text and linguistic commentary—the attack that scholars might have reclassified it as a single 'artificial' language—actually undermine that attack: the polifilesco is analysed as a volgare syntactic base systematically grafted with Latin stems and Greek coinages, i.e. a unified idiolect *formed by* mixing, not a monolingual standard. The acrostic formed by the chapter initials, POLIAM FRATER FRANCISCVS COLVMNA PERAMAVIT, is itself an embedded Latin sentence running through the whole work.
- Journal of the Society of Architectural Historians (peer_reviewed) [Roswitha Stewering, "Architectural Representations in the Hypnerotomachia Poliphili (Aldus Manutius, 1499)," Journal of the Society of Architectural Historians 59, no. 1 (2000): 6-25.]: I opened the 1499 Aldine original (ISTC ic00767000; the edition underlying Pozzi–Ciapponi) rather than the translations swept under the aliases. Against the translation attack: Dallington's English Dream/Strife of Love (1592) and Jean Martin's French Songe de Poliphile (1546) do abridge and normalise the language, but they are translations; the claim is about the work, whose controlling original text the facsimile shows to be hybrid in continuous prose (Latinised and Graecised forms in virtually every sentence of the opening leaves) plus recurring Greek, Latin, Hebrew and Arabic inscriptions and hieroglyphic passages. 'Dense' is justified by the narrative medium itself, not by isolated glosses.

